Title: ZODIAC WARS: THE RISE OF ARIES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Notes: ACT THREE of three, with the post-credit scene. Continues from ACT_2.fountain.

FADE IN:

INT. THE NEXUS STAIR — CONTINUOUS

A spiral of pale stone, descending far further than any building above could hold. The walls are not stone but something older, grained like the inside of a shell, humming. The hum is no longer one note. It is a chord, rising.

Kael and Adaeze go down with their hands still joined, the marks on their wrists lighting the steps red and silver. Behind them, Ima leads Solenne, who walks like a woman in a dream, one hand trailing the wall.

SOLENNE
(vague, pleased)
It's like the Archive. But the wrong way round.

IMA
(fierce, tugging)
Come on.

The stair ends.

INT. THE NEXUS — CONTINUOUS

A vault with no visible ceiling. At the heart of it, hanging in nothing, nine great RINGS of light turn about one another, each a different colour: fire-red, sea-blue, wind-silver, metal-grey, sand-gold. Those five are lit and turning. The other four are dark: white, black, violet, and a pale clear nothing. They hang motionless, like a clock that is missing its hands.

Around the rings, four tall DOORS stand in the dark at the four quarters. They are closed. They are carved with nothing, and they look like absence.

At the centre, on a platform of black stone, is a socket in the shape of a bell. Beside it, Ossara. He holds the bronze tongue in both hands and is bleeding at the lip. Kael's blows have cost him. He does not seem to mind.

He raises the tongue and strikes the platform. The note rings out. The five lit rings flare and one of them, the red, speeds up. On the vault wall, in light, an image: Mars, a grey fleet rising from the Ridge, black ships.

OSSARA
(to the room, calmly)
Louder.

He strikes again. Another image: Venus, Mercury, Saturn. Dark fleets lifting from twelve worlds at once.

At his shoulder the Unbled stands, wounds open and dry, one hand resting lightly on his shoulder like a man steadying a student.

KAEL
(from the stair)
Stop.

Ossara turns. He doesn't look surprised.

OSSARA
You came.

ADAEZE
The Hall is open. The people consented. It is not yours.

OSSARA
I know. I could hear them from here. It was very moving.
(to Kael)
She's been teaching you well.

KAEL
Nobody taught me. She's just right.

OSSARA
(a ghost of a smile)
So was Orla.

He lifts the tongue. The Unbled leans in. The Nexus pulses.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Every strike is a fleet, Kael. Twelve of them, twelve Houses, twelve shadows. When the ringing is done they will rise, and the argument between the Light and the Shadow will be over. I'll pay for every ship. I've counted them.

KAEL
You've counted. You haven't paid.

OSSARA
I will.

KAEL
When? After?

OSSARA
(softly)
After is when it's affordable.

He brings the tongue down. The note this time is enormous. The whole vault convulses. The four dark rings shiver, and from the space between them a cold current moves outward, a draft in a sealed room.

THE UNBLED lifts its head. THE SWORN steps out of the stone beside the stair, gown written in black. THE DROWNED rises out of the floor in a column of weed and slow water.

Three shadows, three Knights.

SOLENNE
(blankly)
Oh, look. More company.

THE DROWNED
(softly, to her)
You don't have to remember them.

IMA
(planting herself between)
Yes, she does.

She lets go of her grandmother's hand, steps forward onto the black stone, and begins to speak. Her voice shakes, but she is clear.

IMA (CONT'D)
Before there were twelve, there were four.

THE DROWNED
Child. Don't.

IMA
They did not have names, because they did not need to be told apart from the world.

SOLENNE
(a flicker)
One held what is carried across a death.

IMA
(surprised, delighted)
Yes!

SOLENNE
(the memory rushing in like water through a broken wall)
One held the distance that lets two things be separate. One held the room that lets anything be made. And one held the whole of it, so none of the others had to.

She straightens. Her eyes clear. She looks at Ima, and her face crumples with joy and sorrow together.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
Ima. My tide. My little tide.

IMA
(running into her arms)
You forgot me.

SOLENNE
I did. I'm so sorry. You told it back to me.

THE DROWNED
(its stone face working)
That's not how it was meant to go.

SOLENNE
(still holding Ima)
No. It's better. A story only has to be told to a child. That's the whole of the rule. The child can be the one who tells it.

She turns to face the Drowned.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
You offered me a ledger of losses. I'd like to give you a ledger of the lucky.

She begins to name them. Not all at once. One at a time, in her archivist's voice: names of people long dead, the sweet-seller on the Neptune quay, her mother's cousin, a poet, a boy who drowned at nine, a girl she taught to read. Each name lands like a stone dropped into a pool, and where it lands the Drowned's smooth face ripples.

THE DROWNED
(cracking)
Stop. Stop.

SOLENNE
No.

She keeps going. The weed falls from its shoulders. The stone face dissolves into sea-water and slides back into the floor, and in the last moment of it a voice, almost grateful, whispers:

THE DROWNED (V.O.)
Memory so deep nothing surfaces.

SOLENNE
Everything surfaces, my dear. You just have to hold the other end.

It is gone.

CUT TO:

INT. THE NEXUS — SAME MOMENT

Across the vault, the Sworn has drifted toward Adaeze, not threatening, just near. She holds out a hand with a scroll in it. Adaeze takes it. Unrolls it. Clause thirty-three, in perfect script.

THE SWORN
The Hall belongs to the one who holds the seat. The boy stands against it. The law is unambiguous. Take his side and you break the one thing that has kept twelve Houses at peace for twelve thousand years.

ADAEZE
(reading, slowly)
Clause thirty-three.

THE SWORN
Yes.

ADAEZE
Do you know who wrote it?

THE SWORN
The Accord.

ADAEZE
No. The Accord wrote a different sentence. This one was added at the Second Convocation by a House that was losing a war and wanted the Court on its side.
(looks up)
It has not had a reason for nine thousand years. It has had a use.

THE SWORN
(softly)
Use is the only reason law survives.

ADAEZE
Then the law is a weapon.

THE SWORN
It is whatever it needs to be.

ADAEZE
(quietly)
No. That's the Shadow's version. The Light's version is: it is whatever we say it is, together, in daylight.

She tears Clause thirty-three in half.

The Sworn recoils. The written gown ripples, lines of text flickering and failing.

THE SWORN
You cannot unwrite the Accord.

ADAEZE
I'm not unwriting it. I'm amending it.

She pulls out her notebook, tears out a page, takes the coloured pencil from behind her ear, and writes a single line. Holds it up for the vault to see, reading as she writes:

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
"No clause of this Accord stands unless the people it binds can read the reason for it."

The page glows silver. The Sworn's gown goes blank, line by line, the black text sliding off her like water. What remains is just a veil, then a pale outline, then nothing.

THE SWORN
(a last whisper; almost relief)
Yes.

She is gone. Adaeze looks at the page in her hand. She is trembling.

ADAEZE
(to herself)
I changed the law.

CUT TO:

INT. THE NEXUS — SAME MOMENT

The platform. Kael, Ossara, the Unbled. Kael has circled to the foot of the black stone. His hands are empty.

OSSARA
You can't take it from me. Not without hurting me. And you don't hurt people.

KAEL
I've hurt you twice.

OSSARA
Once. And you stopped.

KAEL
I'm not here to hurt you, Vahn.

OSSARA
(amused)
Then what are you here for?

KAEL
To make you say it.

OSSARA
I've said it.

KAEL
The whole thing. Every name on that list.

OSSARA
(the smile slipping)
It's forty thousand names.

KAEL
Then it'll take a while.

OSSARA
There are fleets in the sky, boy.

KAEL
Then say it fast.

The Unbled shifts behind Ossara, its stillness suddenly alert.

OSSARA
(unfolding the list)
You want me to read it.

KAEL
Aloud. To the Nexus. To the thing standing behind you.

OSSARA
(a thin laugh)
It doesn't need a reading.

KAEL
It does. It's been eating the ones you don't say.

Ossara holds the list in both hands. For the first time, his hands are not steady.

OSSARA
...Forty thousand.

KAEL
Start.

OSSARA
(reading, flat)
Ansel Dorrin. Maren Cole. Joss Adeyemi...

A pause. Kael says nothing. Ossara reads on. Fifty names. A hundred. The Unbled has taken a step back.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Eleni Okafor. Tomas Vey. The child they called Little Pip.

His voice catches. Stops.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
She was six.

KAEL
Keep going.

OSSARA
(tears he doesn't wipe)
I can't.

KAEL
You can. You've been able to the whole time.

The Unbled steps forward, one of its open wounds brushing Ossara's cheek, and the edges of it begin to blur, to shiver, to fill. A drop of dark red wells in one gash and slides down the shadow's arm.

It is the first thing the Unbled has ever bled.

OSSARA
(stunned; staring at it)
Oh.

The Unbled looks at its own arm. It makes a sound like a great stone moving. Then it staggers backwards, fading.

Ossara drops to his knees. The bronze tongue falls from his hands and rolls across the stone. Kael steps up and picks it up.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
(hoarse)
Forty thousand and one.

KAEL
What?

OSSARA
Orla. I never counted her.

KAEL
(quietly)
Count her.

Ossara bows his head. The vault is quiet. Then:

OSSARA
(a whisper)
Orla Tevane. Master of the Ares Foundry. Eleven thousand pours. The cost was her.

He folds his hands over his face and weeps.

CUT TO:

INT. THE NEXUS — SAME MOMENT

The note of the Nexus drops, as the red ring slows. For a moment, everyone in the vault believes it is over. Ima breathes out. Solenne holds her. Adaeze takes a step toward Kael.

A COLD AIR passes through the room.

Something is wrong with the light. The dark rings, white, black, violet, clear, have begun to turn. Not in time with the others. Against them. Their colours seep outward into the lit rings and wherever they touch, the colour drains. Red goes grey. Blue goes grey. A seamless, even grey.

Sentinel's tiny voice, from the comm-bead at Kael's collar, barely audible:

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Mr Marren, there is no perimeter here.

KAEL
What do you mean?

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Whatever is in this room, it is not inside any bounds I recognise.

The air in the centre of the vault folds. There is nothing there, and the nothing has a shape, then a presence, then a sound.

It speaks.

THE NULL (V.O.)
(not one voice; the unmade voice of everyone who has ever stopped caring who did what)
Hello.

Silence. Everyone in the vault stops moving.

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
You rang the bell.

Ossara lifts his head. His face is raw.

OSSARA
...You were not meant to be here.

THE NULL (V.O.)
No one is meant to be anywhere. That is the relief of me.

(it turns, gently, to Ossara)

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Vahn.

OSSARA
(a whisper)
Yes.

THE NULL (V.O.)
You did not kill her.

OSSARA
(shaking)
I —

THE NULL (V.O.)
The war killed her. You were its instrument. An instrument is not responsible for what it plays.

OSSARA
(longing, horror)
That's not —

THE NULL (V.O.)
It is what you have wanted to hear since the moment she fell. I am only saying it.

Ossara takes a step toward it. The grey is on his hands now.

KAEL
Vahn. Don't.

THE NULL (V.O.)
(turning)
Mediator.

ADAEZE
(rigid)
No.

THE NULL (V.O.)
You wrote a clause. It bound a city. You tore another. It bound a Court. You cannot tell, any more, which of them was the law.

ADAEZE
(whisper)
No.

THE NULL (V.O.)
I can tell you which. None. Put it down.

Adaeze's pencil drops. She has taken a step forward without noticing. Her face is unguarded, nakedly relieved.

THE NULL (V.O.)
(to Solenne)
Archivist. You carried them all. You may stop.

SOLENNE
(low, tempted)
Yes.

IMA
Grandmother.

SOLENNE
It is very heavy, love.

THE NULL (V.O.)
And Knight.

Kael does not move.

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Kael Marren. Foundry apprentice. Counter of costs.

KAEL
...

THE NULL (V.O.)
You hid.

The word lands in the vault like a stone in a well.

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
She died, and you were forty feet away and you did not move. You held a girl's mouth shut so she would not scream. You told yourself it was strategy. You told yourself it was the cost.

(softly)

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It was fear. And you have been counting ever since to prove it was something else.

Kael's face is white. His hands shake. Behind him the others are slowly, helplessly, drifting toward the grey.

THE NULL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I will tell you the true cost, Kael. Nothing. It cost nothing. You were a boy and the world was too large for you. Put it down. That is all I ask. Put it down, and you will not have to say it again.

Kael closes his eyes. When he opens them they are wet, but they are clear.

KAEL
I hid.

THE NULL (V.O.)
Yes.

KAEL
(louder)
I hid. She died. I held Tamsin's mouth shut and I did not move. That's the cost.

THE NULL (V.O.)
It is a small cost. Put it —

KAEL
No.

(He steps forward, onto the black stone, the bronze tongue in his hand.)

KAEL (CONT'D)
It isn't small. It isn't nothing. It isn't a number you can step over. It's mine.

THE NULL (V.O.)
It hurts.

KAEL
Yes. That's how I know it's real.

(He looks at the grey creeping across Ossara, Adaeze, Solenne.)

KAEL (CONT'D)
Orla taught me to say it so that when the pour kills someone, I'm standing beside the body. Not behind a number. Not behind a war. Not behind you.

THE NULL (V.O.)
(softly)
Someone always puts it down.

KAEL
Not today.

He raises the tongue and, with both hands, brings it down on the black stone.

The note is not loud. It is clear. A single pure stroke, exactly nine point one seven one seven, and it rings through the vault like a question asked in an empty room.

The grey freezes.

Then, slowly, the colours return. Red ring, blue ring, silver, grey-iron, gold, each flushing back to its own hue. Ossara gasps, hands rising to his face. Adaeze stoops and picks up her pencil. Solenne puts her arm around Ima.

THE NULL (V.O.)
(very faint)
That was not in the sequence.

KAEL
No.

THE NULL (V.O.)
No one has refused me.

KAEL
Then you've been asking the wrong people.

The cold current thins, swirls, withdraws. The folded air in the centre of the vault smooths over like a pond after a stone. The four dark rings slow. They do not light, but they stop.

A last whisper, almost fond:

THE NULL (V.O.)
I will remember you.

And it is gone.

INT. THE NEXUS — A MOMENT LATER

Silence. The five lit rings turn slowly, steadily. The red ring has slowed to a gentle orbit. On the wall, the visions of the fleets flicker, and fade one by one. Twelve worlds, twelve hangars, twelve ships, now grounded.

Ossara kneels where he fell. Adaeze goes to him and kneels, too.

ADAEZE
Regent Vahn Ossara. You are in the Court's custody.

OSSARA
(raw)
On what authority?

ADAEZE
The people of Meridian. They consented to the opening of the Hall. I think they'd consent to this.

OSSARA
(nodding)
I'll stand trial.

ADAEZE
You'll be heard. By anyone who wants to.

OSSARA
(a broken smile)
That's a better ending than I deserve.

ADAEZE
It isn't an ending.

Ima, holding Solenne's hand, steps up to Kael and looks at the tongue.

IMA
Is it still singing?

KAEL
(listening)
Yes.

IMA
What does it say?

Kael closes his eyes.

KAEL
(after a moment)
It's asking.

IMA
Asking what?

KAEL
What happens next.

Overhead, the vault trembles one last time. Light pours in from the stair. A sound is rising from above: thousands of voices, then bells.

A dry growl from the dark, half amusement, half something close to respect.

ARES (V.O.)
I pressed Mars toward initiative for twelve thousand years.

Kael doesn't look up.

ARES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I did not expect it to rise by counting.

KAEL
(quietly)
It didn't. It just stopped not counting.

ARES (V.O.)
(a grudging chuckle)
Then it begins.

A second voice, silvery, cool:

HERA (V.O.)
It begins well.

A third, sea-deep:

POSEIDON (V.O.)
It begins remembered.

Three lights, red, silver, blue, rise through the vault and out into the day.

EXT. MERIDIAN CITY, THE PLAZA OF THE COURT — EVENING

The crowd has not gone home. Thousands of people stand in the plaza, quiet, as the light fades. The great Court doors are open. At the top of the steps, Kael, Adaeze, Solenne and Ima emerge into the golden evening.

A hush. Then somebody begins to clap. Then someone else. A ragged, tentative, enormous applause that builds without ever quite becoming a cheer.

Kael doesn't know what to do with his hands. Adaeze nudges him.

ADAEZE
(sotto)
Wave.

KAEL
I'm not going to wave.

ADAEZE
Then say something.

KAEL
(to the plaza; small voice, carried by the stillness)
She was Orla Tevane. She ran the Ares Foundry for forty years.
(pause)
I'd like it to be remembered.

The applause dies away, replaced by something quieter and more solid. In the third row, the old woman who said yes first, lifts a hand.

OLD WOMAN
We'll remember.

The sound spreads. A low murmur that is also, slowly, a number: people saying her name. Orla Tevane. Orla Tevane. Not a chant. A counting.

Behind him, Adaeze quietly takes the brass hand-bell out of her pocket and rings it once.

EXT. THE ARES FOUNDRY, OLYMPUS RIDGE — MARS — DAWN (WEEKS LATER)

A cold dawn. The Ridge is quiet, rebuilt in places, scarred in others. A new bell hangs in the foundry yard: the Aries bell, cast but not yet rung, its lip engraved with a single name.

Tamsin stands beside it with Brannock and a handful of journeymen. Kael arrives on foot from the landing apron with a pack on his shoulder and the bronze tongue under his arm, wrapped in new oilcloth.

TAMSIN
You took your time.

KAEL
Earth is a long way.

TAMSIN
You didn't write.

KAEL
I did, twice.

TAMSIN
(softly)
You said nothing in either.

KAEL
(a pause)
I didn't know the cost.

TAMSIN
(thumping his arm)
Of what?

KAEL
Coming back.

She looks at him. Then she hugs him, hard, with her whole body, and he lets her. Over her shoulder he looks at the bell, at the name on its lip.

BRANNOCK
She'd have said the pour wasn't ready.

KAEL
She'd have been right.

BRANNOCK
She'd also have said ring it anyway.

KAEL
(a ghost of a smile)
She'd have said no such thing.

Brannock grunts. The crew parts. Kael climbs the gantry and fits the tongue into the bell's mouth. He stands a moment, hand on the bronze.

KAEL (CONT'D)
Say the cost.

All the journeymen look at him. He nods at Tamsin.

TAMSIN
(slowly, to the bell)
One bell. One tongue. One master we did not save.

BRANNOCK
And a boy who came home.

KAEL
(a breath)
And all the rest we haven't met yet.

He draws back the tongue and strikes.

The note rings out across the Ridge, across the valley, up through the thin Martian air and out toward the pale sun. In the sky, the seam of light has faded to a single bright thread.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(from every wall of the foundry, softly)
The perimeter holds.

Kael closes his eyes. Opens them.

KAEL
(to himself)
For now.

HOLD on the bell, still humming. Then —

INT. THE NEXUS — NIGHT

The vault, silent, rings turning slowly. Five lit. Four dark. The tongue's echo drifts in from above and settles into the stone.

At the northern quarter, the first of the four doors, the white one, stands closed.

A beat.

It opens a hand's width.

Light spills out, white and cold and, strangely, kind. A hand reaches around the edge of the door, not a man's, not a god's, something older than both. It rests on the frame.

A VOICE, soft, unhurried, a thousand deaths worth of patience:

SOUL (V.O.)
Kael Marren.

The hand taps once on the door.

SOUL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
You counted well. Now count the next.

SMASH CUT TO BLACK.

FADE OUT.

THE END

POST-CREDIT SCENE

INT. OLYMPUS RIDGE, A QUIET CORNER OF THE FOUNDRY — NIGHT

Empty. A single oil lamp. On the cold floor where Orla died, a stain remains, dried and dark.

Beside it, a fresh drop of something wet.

Red.

It falls from nowhere, onto the stone, catching the lamp.

The air above the stain shimmers. A faint outline: tall, lean, draped in ember. The Unbled, stooped, one arm held out, watching a single wound beading, for the first time, with blood.

It looks at the drop. Its shape trembles.

ARES (V.O.)
(from the cold furnace, very low)
Interesting.

CUT TO BLACK.

SUPER: "TO BE CONTINUED."
